{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "Word of the tower clock traveled faster than either of them expected. By winter, letters arrived addressed not to the clockmaker but to Mira herself, asking whether she might look at a stalled mantel clock, a music box that played only half its tune, or a pocket watch that had belonged to someone's great-grandfather. She read each one aloud at the bench, and the old man listened without comment, polishing a crystal that did not need polishing. On the first cold morning of the new year, she arrived to find the shop unlocked but empty. A single envelope rested on her stool, weighted down with his favorite loupe. Inside was a key, a list of customers in his cramped handwriting, and one line at the bottom: The rest you already know. She sat for a long time in the ticking quiet, surrounded by dozens of clocks that all seemed to be waiting for her. Later she learned he had gone to live with his sister by the sea, where the air was kind to his lungs and there was nothing to repair but fishing nets. He wrote to her twice a month, never asking about the business, only about what she had discovered. Mira kept the loupe on a cord around her neck. In spring she hung a small wooden sign beneath the old one, painted with her own name. The first customer to notice it was a boy of about eleven, who pressed his face to the window and watched her work until his mother called him home."}
